hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 58
--- Psalm 58 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 58
sub: Hard preaching • A God that judgeth in the earth
refs: Psalm 58:1

--- Hard preaching
kicker: Psalm 58 · Imprecatory prayer
p: **Psalm 58** serves not only as another example of imprecatory prayer, but as it's meant to be read or sung among the congregation, it serves as an example of hard preaching.
p: Leather lung, face-ripping, red-hot preaching. Hard preaching by the prophets of old, holy men moved by the Spirit, speaking directly to the sin among the people, is a Biblical standard. It is God's desire for man to be pricked by the Spirit and led to the fear of the Lord by it.

--- Peace, peace
kicker: Application • Hard preaching
p: Those who hate hard preaching, who seek to be soothed from behind the pulpit with sermons of peace, peace, when there is no peace...They aren't right with God.
p: Either you are convicted towards repentance and acknowledgement to God of your sin, or you have already seen the wisdom of it, and have enough fear of God and discernment to shout "Amen!"

--- O congregation
kicker: Psalm 58 · Do ye indeed speak righteousness?
big: Psalm 58:1
p: David opens with a scathing rhetorical question. He is calling them out. You play church and are there when the assembly is called out, but are you there spiritually? Do you actually have the judgment your piousness is meant to indicate?

--- In heart ye work wickedness
kicker: Psalm 58 · The violence of your hands
big: Psalm 58:2 | mark: in heart ye work wickedness
p: The heart is desperately wicked. If you aren't conformed to the will of God for your life, walking in the Spirit so as to not fulfill the lusts of the flesh, seeking the profit of others, judging the poor and afflicted, what are you even doing?
big: Matthew 12:30 | mark: He that is not with me is against me
p: If you aren't on God's program, and aren't working for the Lord to do his will, you are against Him. Hard preaching is there to act as a goad, reminder to those who grow comfortable and complacent. It's also there to call out the wicked deeds of men.

--- Speaking lies
kicker: Psalm 58 · Estranged from the womb
p: David goes on to describe what the wicked are like, simile and metaphor, designed to prick the heart.
big: Psalm 58:3 | mark: speaking lies
p: The wicked seem to go astray from birth, speaking lies.
p: Now some in the Reformed movement take this to teach the wicked heresy of total depravity from the womb. "Vipers in diapers," they call it. David's hyperbolic language is shown to be such, when he says they speak lies. Innocent babies cannot speak lies, they cannot speak.

--- The deaf adder
kicker: Psalm 58 · The voice of charmers
scripture: Psalm 58:4-5 | mark: they are like the deaf adder that stoppeth her ear
p: The poetic point of how the wicked seem to be, continues. Comparing them to the deaf adder, the snake that resists the music of the charmer.
p: In ancient Near East culture, especially among the Egyptians, snake charming was not merely a street performance for the tourists, but a valued service that provided medical care for bites, treatment for venom, and exterminator services to rid the house of the creeping threat.

--- Deaf before the preaching
kicker: Application • Hard preaching
p: The lies of the wicked are as the venom of asps and cobras. And they appear deaf before the preaching of the Lord.
p: There are many who sit among the church pews today. Not hearing the word preached. Who doesn't want to hear the whole counsel of God.
p: David has a plea to God for vengeance. He has a desire they meet His judgment, if not the simple way by hearing, then a visceral way by His power.

--- Break their teeth
kicker: Psalm 58 · The young lions
big: Psalm 58:6
p: They might harken to a curb stomp from God when hard preaching does nothing to their hearts of stone.
p: David continues cursing them and their impenetrable hearts.

--- As a snail which melteth
kicker: Psalm 58 · Let them melt away
scripture: Psalm 58:7-8 | mark: Let them melt away as waters; As a snail which melteth
p: Let them melt away as waters, let their bows be broken, and they be cut to pieces. Let them melt away as the snail in the sun. Let them be as a miscarriage, that they not ever see the Sun.
p: This is hard preaching. How many pastors and preachers back away from it, today? Especially in the age of YouTube and social media clips. Hate campaigns by lewd fellows of the baser sort, stirred up against a local church or its man of God for using similar language against the enemies of God, have led many to silence.
p: Praise God it has led others to double down and endure persecution for His sake.

--- Not rare
kicker: Application • Imprecatory psalms
p: David's Psalms contain wonderful and uplifting messages about the glory of God, the blessedness of the man who walks in His righteousness, the faith in Him to deliver during our toughest times, and the praise God only deserves when He sees us through. Positive preaching, a majority of the time.
p: But imprecatory Psalms, cursing the enemies of God and promising His swift and terrible justice upon them aren't absent. They aren't rare. They come up in the catalogue, often, given as an example. We shouldn't be offended or shocked at such graphic and harsh language. We should never make excuses for sin or give comfort to the workers of iniquity.

--- As with a whirlwind
kicker: Psalm 58 · Before your pots can feel the thorns
big: Psalm 58:9
p: The day of the Lord is coming. Nomads and travelers used dry desert thorns, brambles as kindling for campfires because they caught fire instantly and burned with intense, sudden heat. They'd cook in their pots above the flame, but God's wrath will be so sudden, like a whirlwind upon the wicked, with His wrath poured out.
p: The suddenness of His wrath is a key doctrine in scripture;
big: Proverbs 6:15 | mark: suddenly shall he be broken without remedy

--- Peace and safety
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 58:9
p: The sudden destruction is linked to the soft preaching of peace for the wicked;
big: 1 Thessalonians 5:3 | mark: when they shall say, Peace and safety; then sudden destruction cometh upon them
p: That day will come upon them quickly. Just as it did before the flood.
scripture: Luke 17:26-27 | mark: as it was in the days of Noe

--- As in a moment
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 58:9
p: This truth is foundational. God is a rewarder of those who diligently seek Him, but to those that despise His judgment, swift destruction.
big: Psalm 73:19 | mark: as in a moment
p: We who are His are not meant to shrink from His judgments and wrath, we aren't to be embarrassed by it, or made to feel cowed into denouncing a vengeful God. We are meant to rejoice.

--- A God that judgeth in the earth
kicker: Psalm 58 · The righteous shall rejoice
scripture: Psalm 58:10-11 | mark: The righteous shall rejoice when he seeth the vengeance; verily he is a God that judgeth in the earth
p: Crying out for His wrath upon the ungodly is as natural for the believer as praising God for His longsuffering and mercy. Love requires it.
big: Romans 12:9 | mark: Abhor that which is evil
p: True love and righteous judgment is loving good, hating evil and rejoicing at His judgement.

--- The winepress
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 58:10
p: We wait with patience not only our redemption, but God's judgment against the wicked.
big: Revelation 6:10 | mark: How long, O Lord
p: Jesus Himself will execute the judgment against the wicked. We shall wash our feet in the blood of them He destroys with His holy word.
big: Revelation 14:20 | mark: blood came out of the winepress

--- The same God
kicker: Application • Hard preaching
p: Hellfire and brimstone is as biblical as love thy enemies. Jesus is as much a God of vengeance as the Father. There is no variance in God. Jesus the same yesterday today and forever.
scripture: Proverbs 1:24-26 | mark: I also will laugh at your calamity
p: The Prince of Peace is the same God.
p: So as faithful Christians let us not pull back from hard preaching and imprecatory prayers towards the ungodly. Let us rejoice in the wrath against the wicked, even as we patiently await our salvation. Amen

=== Psalm 59
--- Psalm 59 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 59
sub: When Saul sent, and they watched the house to kill him. Psalm 59 is taught in The Psalms of David's Flight.
refs: Psalm 59:1
read: davids-flight-1-the-flight-begins | The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1

=== Psalm 60
--- Psalm 60 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 60
sub: A banner to them that fear thee • Through God we shall do valiantly
refs: Psalm 60:1

--- David's wars
kicker: Psalm 60 · Syria and Edom
p: **Psalm 60** presents an interesting perspective of events during the reign of King David. It was written to commemorate the time of war, when Joab strove with Syria in the north, or Aram-naharaim and with Aram-zobah as it's called in the superscription, and then returned to help defeat Edom in the South.
p: We are given details of the victories in 2 Samuel 8, 10, and again here in 1 Chronicles 18;
scripture: 1 Chronicles 18:3-7 | mark: Thus the LORD preserved David whithersoever he went

--- The valley of salt
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 60
p: At some point, after these lands were put into subjection, attention was turned to Moab and Edom, south of the Kingdom of Israel.
scripture: 1 Chronicles 18:12-15 | mark: all the Edomites became David’s servants

--- Dedicated unto the LORD
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 60
p: We are told of these decisive wins in a multi front war against all the adversaries of Israel. They defeated the Philistines, taking Gath and the Moabites, who became David’s servants.
p: In the victory against Syria, and the others, David gained territory, tribute and treasure.
scripture: 1 Chronicles 18:7-8; 1 Chronicles 18:11 | mark: wherewith Solomon made the brasen sea; Them also king David dedicated unto the LORD
p: Other Kings, hearing of these feats, turned and offered gifts and tribute, and of this wealth, Solomon built the Temple.

--- The wine of astonishment
kicker: Psalm 60 · Thou hast cast us off
scripture: Psalm 60:1-3 | mark: thou hast cast us off; thou hast made us to drink the wine of astonishment
p: But **Psalm 60** begins on a low note. David cries out that God has cast off His people and turned against their armies. The earth quaked in His displeasure, and David asks to be healed. The King believes that they suffered losses and made the people see the Lord's displeasure by whatever had transpired to have David this despondent. Drunk on the wine of astonishment.
p: This is a vivid allegory, the Bible often uses drunkenness or the effects of wine to illustrate bewilderment or astonishment. David wants God to know, message received.

--- A banner
kicker: Psalm 60 · Because of the truth
scripture: Psalm 60:4-5 | mark: Thou hast given a banner to them that fear thee, that it may be displayed because of the truth
p: The banner of the Lord is for those that fear Him and it is displayed because of the truth. That the power of God be displayed in His aid in the battles that come.
p: That all who see this strength come to fear Him.
p: David asks that Israel be delivered by the strength of the Lord, by His power, in this time of crisis.

--- God hath spoken in his holiness
kicker: Psalm 60 · I will rejoice
scripture: Psalm 60:6-8 | mark: God hath spoken in his holiness; I will rejoice
p: Without the Psalms giving a running journal of David's mind and heart during the events documented in the historical books, we might think that he and his mighty men were an unstoppable war machine creating an empire. Not a meek and lowly man, depending entirely on the strength of an almighty God.
p: *God hath spoken in his holiness; I will rejoice*.
p: It is the humility of David the Psalmist where we see the truth. His dedication to the Lord in confession and meekness, relying on his faith to deliver them. Not his armies and mighty men.

--- Hanun
kicker: Cross references • 2 Samuel 10
p: We are given more details of the war with Syria in 2 Samuel 10:1-7;
scripture: 2 Samuel 10:1-4 | mark: I will shew kindness unto Hanun the son of Nahash

--- They stank before David
kicker: Cross references • 2 Samuel 10
scripture: 2 Samuel 10:5-7 | mark: the children of Ammon saw that they stank before David
p: The Ammonites humiliated the servants of David sent as emissaries of peace, and quickly gathering that they were unwise to have done this, they recruit the armies of Syria to aid them.

--- Be of good courage
kicker: Cross references • 2 Samuel 10
scripture: 2 Samuel 10:8-12 | mark: Be of good courage, and let us play the men for our people; and the LORD do that which seemeth him good

--- That which seemed good
kicker: Psalm 60 · Victory
p: The end result was the defeat of Ammon, the retreat and regathering of Syria, only to see their planned invasion stopped and their armies destroyed.
p: Trusting in the Lord to do that which seemed good, led to all of these military wins.
p: And David made peace with the enemies and established garrisons in their cities, and collected tribute from all the nations who now became his servants.

--- A Star out of Jacob
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 60:8
p: This was a fulfillment of prophecy, made centuries before.
scripture: Numbers 24:17-19 | mark: there shall come a Star out of Jacob; Edom shall be a possession
p: This prophecy was given to the wicked Balaam, and applies here, in the Kingdom of David, as well as the future reign of the King of Kings, from the root of Jesse, the son of David, Jesus Christ.

--- Who will lead me into Edom?
kicker: Psalm 60 · The strong city
scripture: Psalm 60:9-10 | mark: who will lead me into Edom?
p: David defeats Moab and stations garrisons throughout all of Edom so that "*all the Edomites became David's servants*."
p: And when Isaac blessed Jacob (Israel) instead of Esau (the ancestor of the Edomites), he prophesied that nations, specifically Esau's descendants of Edom would serve Jacob:
scripture: Genesis 27:28-29 | mark: Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee

--- He never presumes
kicker: Application • Humility
p: The foretold blessings become a reality under David. But even as David sees the prophecies of old fulfilled by the almighty God, he never presumes. He always approaches the Lord contrite, asking for deliverance. Never assuming that it's his.
p: It's amazing that even in the thick of it, as the scriptures come to life, a man who is so highly favored by the Lord remains humble. And petitions the Lord with prayer, rejoicing in song amongst the congregation.

--- Through God we shall do valiantly
kicker: Psalm 60 · Vain is the help of man
scripture: Psalm 60:11-12 | mark: for vain is the help of man; Through God we shall do valiantly
p: So whatever fear or setbacks they may have had David trusts God to deliver them and He does. Not only bringing great victory and fulfilling prophecy but giving provision for the creation of the temple under Solomon.
p: All things working for good to those that love God. This is the greatest lesson of the Psalms. God provides His people protection and blessings, when they humble themselves and come before Him. Let us have that heart of David. Let us seek God early knowing that he is a rewarder of those that seek him. Amen

